The majority of the to date reported Ti MOFs contain Ti-O clusters as secondary building unit (SBU), but very recently a few chain-based Ti MOFs were reported: DGIST-1, Ti-TBP and MIL-177-HT.[7,11,12] The dimensionality of the SBU greatly influences the MOF properties, as was exemplified by photoconductivity measurements on the chain-based MIL-177-HT and its cluster-based counterpart, MIL-177-LT. The latter showed negligible conductivity whereas MIL-177-HT features similar conductivity as nano-sized $\mathsf{T i O}_{2},$ which demonstrates the benefit of increasing the SBU’s dimensionality to achieve bulk oxide properties.
Herein, we report a new, photoactive, $\mathsf{T i}^{\mathsf{N}}\ 4,4^{\prime}$ - biphenyldicarboxylate (bpdc2-) MOF, termed COK-47, featuring a complex layer of $\Tilde{\Pi}\Tilde{\mathsf{O}}_{6}$ octahedra as 2D SBU. Interestingly, a careful choice of synthetic conditions allows for control over particle morphology and defect content, making, $C0K-47s$ ( $\zeta=$ small particle) a highly active catalyst, e.g. towards the oxidation of dibenzothiophene. Several isoreticular analogues with for instance terephthalate or $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- ${\cdot}5,5^{\prime}$ - dicarboxylate linkers were also synthesized (Figure S1).
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of the obtained $C0K-47s$ comprises only three broad diffraction peaks (Figure 1a, blue trace), which are typically associated with a high degree of disorder or very small crystallite domain size. While both can be desired from a standpoint of catalytic applications, the small number of broad reflections for $C0K-47s$ strongly impedes indexing, structure elucidation and Rietveld refinement from conventional diffraction data. Therefore, a second synthetic procedure was developed to obtain particles of a sufficient quality to resolve COK-47’s structure. The material, further referred to as $C0K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ $(\mathsf{L}\ =$ large particle), features the same three diffraction peaks as observed in ${\mathsf{C O K}}{\cdot}47_{5},$ but of significantly decreased width (Figure 1a, red trace). Additionally, the PXRD pattern of $C0K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ contained some new, broad and overlapping peaks (e.g. at $15^{\circ}$ and $26^{\circ}2\theta$ ). It was unclear if these new peaks corresponded to those from the MOF structure or with impurities like titanium oxyhydroxides; so indexing and subsequent structure solution remained impossible. Given the small size of the crystallites, even in ${\mathsf{C O K}}{\cdot}47_{\mathrm{L}},$ and the ambiguity of the PXRD data, we opted to perform continuous rotation electron diffraction (cRED) to obtain a structural model for COK-47.[13,14] Because electrons interact more strongly than X-rays, they are more suitable for single crystal diffraction experiments on nanocrystalline materials. Figure 1b shows the reconstructed 3D reciprocal lattice observed with cRED performed on a single $C0K-47_{\mathrm{L}}$ crystallite. The collected data could be indexed in the monoclinic space group $c2/c$ (unit cell as refined to PXRD data: $a=6.224\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ; $b=30.183$ Å; $c=7.333\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\beta=106.135^{\circ})$ . Intensities were extracted from cRED data and the structure was solved and refined using SHELXT and SHELXL respectively (for additional details see SI).
The structure of COK-47 consists of Ti-O sheets which are connected by bpdc2- linkers to form a threedimensional framework (Figure 2). The inorganic moiety contains double chains of edge-sharing $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{6}$ octahedra which are interconnected by $\upmu_{2}{-}0$ bridges. Each linker molecule connects with one carboxylate to an edge sharing pair of octahedra, while the other carboxylate binds to a corner sharing $\mathsf{T i O}_{6}$ pair in the next titaniumoxo sheet. The significant dihedral angle between the two linker carboxylates is probably the reason why the synthesis was most easily performed with the slightly twisted H2bpdc linker.
The theoretical powder pattern calculated from this structural model matches well with that of $C O K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ (Figure 1a), but containing much more reflections than $\mathsf{C O K}\mathtt{-}47_{5}$ . A closer look at the differences between both patterns revealed that the $h k I$ reflections with $\scriptstyle{I\neq0}$ are very weak in the experimental pattern of $C O K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ and only the $_{0k0}$ set of planes was observed for ${\mathsf{C O K}}{\cdot}47_{5}$ . This suggests preferred orientation of the powder bed, disorder or smaller crystallite dimensions along specific axes.
The difference in growth rate along the inorganic plane between $C0K-47s$ and $C0K\cdot47_{\circ}$ (Figure 3c-d) can be explained by the diverse synthetic conditions (details in SI): $C0K-47s$ is synthesized with a high reagent concentration at high temperature under microwave irradiation for short reaction times, which typically yields small particles.[15] In addition, the low water content in the $C0K-47s$ synthesis slows down the hydrolysis rate of the Ti precursor and thus the in-plane growth rate, whereas extension along the $b$ -axis is increased by the high linker concentration. This results in a similar crystallite dimension along the $b$ -axis for both synthetic procedures, while the $C0K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ crystallites are much bigger along the $a$ -and $c$ -axes. The distinct morphology control is peculiar and could influence the macroscopic properties, for instance by preferentially exposing certain facets to tune the catalytic activity of the material.[16]
PXRD and TEM confirmed the long range order of $C0K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $C0K-47s$ but the influence of the synthetic method on the local structure remained unclear. Therefore, pair distribution functions (PDFs) were derived from X-ray total scattering measurements and compared to the simulated PDF (Figure 4a). In the low Rrange $({\mathsf{R}}{<}12\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ , nearly all correlations are present in both $C0K\cdot47_{5}$ and $\mathsf{C O K-47_{L}},$ demonstrating that they feature almost the same local structure. The PDF of $C0K\cdot47s$ is however more damped at higher R-values, likely due to disorder in this material. In addition, some peaks in the low R-range are slightly shifted $(\approx0.1{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}})$ compared to these of $C0K-47_{\circ}$ and the theoretical PDF, indicating minor differences in the $C0K-47s$ structure. These differences are situated in the Ti layers so a detailed investigation on the local environment of Ti was performed via extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) measurements of the Ti K-edge (Figure 4b). A good fit (R-factor $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=0.03$ ) was obtained for $C O K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ and there are indeed some distinct differences with the EXAFS spectrum of $C0K\cdot47s$ . The first maximum at $1{-}2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in the real part is split in two peaks for the latter material, indicating some rearrangement in the nearest Ti-O shell. The similar intensity of this maximum however demonstrates that Ti remains six fold coordinated. A second difference is the absence of a shoulder at $2.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (phase uncorrected) in the $C0K-47s$ spectrum. This peak is associated with a Ti-Ti pair on which a carboxylate is attached so the change is likely associated with missing linker defects.
Missing linkers defects are widely studied in MOFs and often deliberately introduced because they are associated with open metal sites, which are potentially catalytically active, and a change in porosity. In the case of COK-47, the structural model does not contain micropores, and indeed the measured BET surface area of $C0K-47_{\mathrm{L}}$ is only $60~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ ${\mathsf{C O K}}{\cdot}{4}{7}_{5}$ however displays a stunning BET surface area of $573\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ of which $100\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ was fitted to be external surface area (Figure S15); its pore size calculated from the nitrogen isotherm is $6\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ which is in agreement with the theoretical pore size of the COK-47 structure featuring a missing linker (Figure S17).
The presence of other molecules on these linker defects of ${\mathsf{C O K}}–47_{5}$ was investigated via $^1\mathsf{H}$ liquid NMR after digestion with HF (Figure S18). Both modulator and cyclopentadiene were completely absent from the $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum, but besides the H2bpdc linker, a large amount of methanol was present for ${\mathsf{C O K}}{\cdot}47_{5},$ which was almost completely absent in COK-47L’s solution. Since the materials were evacuated under vacuum at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ prior to digestion, physisorbed methanol in the pores could be excluded and the lack of methanol in $C0K-47_{\mathrm{L}}$ was attributed to its pristine nature. $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ and $^1\mathsf{H}$ MAS NMR of $C0K-47s$ revealed the presence of methoxides, which have been widely observed as capping agents both on discrete ${\mathsf{T i}}^{\mathsf{N}}$ clusters, on the ${\bar{\mathsf{T i}}}^{\mathsf{N}}$ cluster in MOF-901 and on $Z r$ -MOFs like UiO-66 (Figure S20).[10,17,18] As in the latter example, the methoxides in $C0K\cdot47_{5}$ stem from reversible post-synthetic exchange with hydroxyl groups during washing (Figure S21).
The nature of these methoxides was investigated by constructing and DFT optimizing four different defective models (Figure 5a). Inclusion of the $C H_{3}$ -moieties most likely occurs on missing linker defects so the first two models comprised the replacement of a linker by a bridging (model 1) or terminal (model 2) methoxide. The latter model also contains an open site on the neighboring Ti atom. Model 3 and 4 were included to check whether methoxides could replace respectively $\upmu_{2}$ or $\upmu_{3}$ framework oxygens. After DFT optimization, GIPAW (Gauge-Including Projector Augmented Waves) calculations were performed and the calculated chemical shifts were compared with the experimental $^{13}{\mathsf C}$ NMR spectra (Figure 5b). The shifts of the linker carbons in all models agreed well with the experimental data but there were large differences in the calculated methoxidecarbon shifts. Interestingly, the measured methoxide peak of $C0K-47s$ also shifts (from 63 ppm to 77 ppm) when increasing the activation temperature from $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The shift of the bridging methoxide in model 1 (62 ppm) coincided perfectly with the measured spectrum after activation at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ whereas the calculated shift of the terminal methoxide in model 2 (74 ppm) aligned with the $C H_{3}$ -peak after activation at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}.$ -shifts of the methoxides in model 3 (87 and 94 ppm) were much higher so replacement of a $\upmu_{2}{-}0$ is deemed unlikely. Similarly, the terminal methoxide in model 4 $(84~\mathsf{p p m})$ was shifted too high and missing $\upmu_{3}–0$ defects can therefore be excluded. We can thus conclude that the missing linker defects of $C0K\cdot47s$ are capped by bridging methoxides after evacuation at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and that high temperature activation at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ results in the formation of terminal methoxides with neighboring open sites.
The spatial distribution of the methoxides was studied in depth via $^1\mathsf{H}$ spin-diffusion NMR, a technique which probes the proximity of organic moieties (Figure 5c-d).[19,20] The intense cross-peaks between the methoxide protons (resonating at 4.2 ppm) and the linker carbon nuclei (resonating between 120 and $180~\mathsf{p p m})$ in the spectrum demonstrate that the methoxides and linkers are in close proximity. The measured spindiffusion rate of the sample activated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ resembles well the calculated one of model 1, whereas the simulation for model 2 fits the experimental diffusion kinetics of the sample activated at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure 5c). This provides additional evidence for the temperature induced transition from bridging to terminal methoxides and also demonstrates that the defects are homogeneously distributed through the material.[19] The experimentally determined number of methoxides $(\mathsf{M e O}/\mathsf{b p d c}\ =\ 1.3)$ in $C0K-47s$ corresponds with $39\%$ missing linkers, an exceptionally high number that is only possible because the 2D inorganic moieties are theoretically linked by an infinite number of dicarboxylates. The chemical composition $([\mathsf{T i}_{2}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O})_{2}(\upmu_{2}-$ $\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{b p d c})_{0.61}(\mathsf{M e O})_{0.78}])$ matches well with the loss of organic matter $(50\%)$ during decomposition at $440^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , as determined by thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S24).
The presence of large numbers of methoxide groups and open sites in $C0K-47s$ suggests the availability of Lewis acidity (LA), especially since the former can easily be replaced by reactive species.[17] LA sites would imbue the material with catalytic activity, and for $C0K-47s$ this was explored using the oxidation of dibenzothiophene (DBT) (Figure 6a). Oxidative desulfurization (ODS) is a mild, industrially relevant alternative for hydrodesulfurization since the latter requires high hydrogen pressures to remove thiophenes in order to meet the ever more stringent requirements for combustion fuels.[21] The reaction is known to be catalyzed by a range of Ti-containing materials including hierarchical titanium silicates and MIL-125, but also by other MOFs such as the vanadium-based MIL-47 and the zirconium-based UiO-66.[22–24]
Reaction was performed with $15\mathrm{\mol\%}$ catalyst and 2.5 eq. tert-butylhydroperoxide (TBHP) as oxidant in toluene at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ Without catalyst, only $2\%$ conversion was obtained after $24\ h$ while $C0K-47s$ converted $99\%$ of DBT within two hours (Figure 6b). GC/MS confirmed full selectivity to the corresponding sulfone. No conversion occurred when TBHP was omitted. The reaction follows pseudo-first order kinetics and the apparent rate constant for $\mathsf{C O K-}47_{5}(41.1\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ exceeds that of all other tested catalysts. $\mathsf{C O K}\mathsf{-}47_{\mathsf{L}}\left(3.6\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\right)$ is eleven times less active because it contains almost no open sites and has a much lower surface area. Similarly, MIL-125 $(0.8\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ displays very limited activity because it lacks defective sites and the low activity of Degussa P25 $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ $(<0.1\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ can be explained by the limited surface area of the latter. UiO-66 with two missing-linker defects per $Z r_{6}$ cluster $(2.7\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ and MOF-808 (4.3 $\textsf{x}10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n^{-1}}$ ) which contains six open sites per cluster, showed reaction rates still an order of magnitude lower than for ${\tt C O K-47_{5}}$ . MIL-47 $(11.9\times10^{-3}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ lacks open sites but likely acts as a homogeneous catalyst since it was observed to degrade during reaction.[25] Oxidation of other thiophenes such as benzothiophene (BT) was also performed but the reaction is slower because BT is a less electron rich substrate, making it an inherently less reactive substrate (Figure S29).[26] ICP analysis indicated less than 1 ppm Ti in solution after DBT oxidation, which together with a hot filtration test confirmed the heterogeneity of the $\mathsf{C O K-47_{S}}$ . Its stability was furthermore established via PXRD and a recycling test (Figure S30-31). The reaction mechanism is believed to proceed through the coordination of TBHP to the LA open sites, thereby replacing the capping methoxides, which was demonstrated by the detection of methanol in the solution after reaction.
Development of new Ti MOFs is often driven by expectations about their redox properties and photoactivity.[27] Irradiation of $C0K-47s$ or $C O K\cdot47_{\mathrm{L}}$ with UV-A in ethanol under inert atmosphere resulted in a color change from a white to a dark blue powder, indicating a valence change from ${\bar{\mathsf{T i}}}^{\mathsf{N}}$ to ${\mathsf{T i}}^{\mathsf{I I I}}$ , while maintaining its structural integrity (Figure S34). In addition, a strong signal with $g$ -values $\displaystyle(g_{\perp}=1.932$ and $g_{\lvert{\lvert{\begin{array}{c}{\begin{array}{r l}\end{array}}}\end{array}}}$ $\mathbf{\delta}=1.899\$ ) corresponding to octahedrally coordinated TiIII appears in the electron paramagnetic (EPR) spectrum (Figure 7).[28,29]
When the photoreduced $C0K\cdot47_{5}$ is exposed to air, the EPR signal and blue color disappear within minutes, thus confirming the reversibility of the reduction. The change in valence state of ${\bar{\mathsf{T i}}}^{\mathsf{N}}$ to $\mathsf{T i}^{\mathsf{|||}}$ is expected to be compensated by replacement of oxo through hydroxo bridges in the inorganic moiety, as has been previously demonstrated for COK-69.[28] The photocatalytic activity of $C0K-47s$ was studied via the degradation of a fluorescent dye, rhodamine 6G (Rh6G) (Figure S35). COK$47_{5}$ fully decomposes Rh6G upon UV exposure, while maintaining its structural stability (Figure S36). The degradation rate is similar as in a reaction catalyzed by a conventional $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ photocatalyst and higher than for MIL125. This demonstrates the photocatalytic potential of $C0K-47s$ and paves the way for further applications.
In conclusion, we synthesized the first titanium carboxylate MOF exhibiting a sheet-based structure, COK-47, and by selecting appropriate synthesis conditions, a highly defective material was obtained. The resulting $C0K-47s$ is porous, featuring bridging or terminal methoxides, and shows excellent catalytic activity for the oxidation of dibenzothiophene. $C0K-47s$ can reversibly be reduced and degrades Rh6G upon UV irradiation indicating its potential in (photo)catalytic applications.
